    <type>camp</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="31.686030" lon="-110.973480">
    <name>Forest Road 143 / Salero Canyon Rd</name>
    <desc>The surface and difficulty of Forest Road 143/Salero Canyon Road change year-to-year due to monsoons and the way the road is maintained. While the road offers some camping opportunities, incredible views of the Santa Cruz River Valley, and a vast diversity of fauna (a prickly pear cactus next to a cedar tree), this road has a reputation for crushing tired souls who are not mentally prepared for the persistent steep grades and loose surfaces. The rough and tough stuff lasts for approximately 12 miles. Plan accordingly! [Sky Islands Odyssey West waypoint; route km 1237]</desc>
    <sym>Campground</sym>
    <type>camp</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="31.687740" lon="-110.973460">
    <name>Forest Service Road 143 / Salero Canyon Road</name>
    <desc>The surface and difficulty of Forest Road 143/Salero Canyon Road change year-to-year due to monsoons and the way the road is maintained. While the road offers some camping opportunities, incredible views of the Santa Cruz River Valley, and a vast diversity of fauna (a prickly pear cactus next to a cedar tree), this road has a reputation for crushing tired souls who are not mentally prepared for the persistent steep grades and loose surfaces. The rough and tough stuff lasts for approximately 12 miles. Plan accordingly! [Sky Islands Odyssey East waypoint; route km 1237]</desc>
    <sym>Campground</sym>
    <type>camp</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="31.704000" lon="-110.932880">
    <name>Wilderness Boundary Ahead</name>
    <desc>The route turns west here. If you wish to explore the Quantrell Mine trail by ascending further (which would be an out-and-back), do so on foot because bikes are not permitted in wilderness areas. [Madrean Rugged Ramble waypoint; route km 1244]</desc>
    <sym>Information</sym>
    <type>info</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="31.739150" lon="-110.892550">
    <name>Proctor Rd Dispersed Camping</name>
    <desc>[Madrean Rugged Ramble waypoint; route km 1255]</desc>
    <sym>Campground</sym>
    <type>camp</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="31.738500" lon="-110.885410">
    <name>Madera Canyon</name>
    <desc>Just slightly off route is Madera Canyon, one of the most famous birding areas in the United States. It is a north-facing valley in the Santa Rita Mountains with riparian woodland along an intermittent stream, bordered by mesquite, juniper-oak woodlands, and pine forests. Madera Canyon is home to over 250 species of birds, including 15 hummingbird species. Visitors from all over the world search for avian specialties such as the Elegant Trogon, Elf Owl, Sulphur-bellied Flycatcher, Red-faced Warbler, and Painted Redstart. A comprehensive bird list published by FoMC is available at the Proctor Visitor Info Station and at trailheads. (https://friendsofmaderacanyon.org/) [Sky Islands Odyssey East waypoint; route km 1256]</desc>
    <sym>Information</sym>
    <type>info</type>
  </wpt>
